# EXPECT: as a for binder
#
# A nested define that declares `local x` and also uses x as a `for` binder.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE is a VM scoping rule that depends on nesting depth
# and binding kind — and the first guard written from it keyed on the WRONG
# AXIS. It refused "a dominating top-level local used as a binder"; the VM's
# rule is that in a nested define the binder is loop-scoped for EVERY binding
# kind except a parameter. Deleting the word `local` from this very fixture
# evaded that guard (its sibling covers the plain-`is` spelling now), and four
# more spellings diverged silently — through a capture (VM 108 / AOT 305) and
# over a module global, which the AOT clobbered. Measured rows:
#
#   binder over a `local` in a NESTED define     loop-scoped  (x stays 7 after)
#   binder over the same local, TOP-LEVEL define persists     (x becomes 2)
#   binder over a nested define's PARAMETER      persists
#
# The AOT emits one C variable in every case, so the nested-local shape
# silently diverged: VM 15, AOT 10, both build, both rc 0. Worse, for the
# variant with an enclosing `local x` two commits earlier had REFUSED loudly —
# the local-shadow exemption train converted a loud refusal into silence.
#
# Same text, three meanings by context: the fourth road-dependence instance
# this loop has sent upstream (EigenScript#1056).

define f(y) as:
    define b(d) as:
        local x is 7
        for x in [1, 2]:
            d is d + x
        return d + x
    return b of 5

print of (f of 0)
